          "secNumber": "2.2",
          "heading": "Unit perspective",
          "content": "Hillslopes receive and produce material through chemical alteration, physical breakdown, bioturbation, rockfall and mass movement. Soil thickness is a state variable governed by production from bedrock, surface transport, deposition and removal. A thick soil layer need not mean a stable old surface: rapid production and supply may coexist with rapid erosion. Conversely, a bare rock slope can persist because weathering is slow, transport is rapid or rockfalls expose fresh surfaces. The manner in which material moves depends on slope geometry, grain size, vegetation, water pressure, cohesion and time. Slow hillslope diffusion is a useful long-term approximation where many small disturbances accumulate. It cannot capture a large debris flow, which may initiate abruptly and move far beyond the source slope. We must keep continuum descriptions distinct from event-driven failures. Even a textbook factor-of-safety calculation is an idealized balance, not an operational landslide prediction. For conservation, housing and road design, geology must be supplemented by site measurements, engineering assessment and an explicit treatment of uncertainty.\n\n### Learning outcomes and scale discipline\n\nBy the end of this unit, students should be able to describe at least five independent landform or sediment observations, demonstrate one dimensionally consistent quantitative calculation, design a comparative measurement, and distinguish an interpretation from the observations used to support it. Practice viewing the same landform in a map, a section and a material description. Keep an explicit column for phenomena that are absent, unobserved or only assumed. At the finest scale, a few grain contacts may govern transport; at larger scales, inherited valley geometry and event histories control the response. Switching scale is legitimate only after specifying which variables remain meaningful. At the start of the field exercise, make a drawing of soil depth along one crest-to-hollow traverse and timed tracer distances; list which parts would be measured directly and which reconstructed from other evidence.\n\nThe unit's measurement choices should include bedrock exposure, soil composition, slope angle and water conditions; the strongest report also checks parent-rock identity and direct soil contacts. Even a carefully derived rate does not establish its formative mechanism unless the sedimentary or stratigraphic evidence agrees. Interpret changes in the context of the process and observation interval, and use at least two sources of independent evidence. Most useful scientific uncertainties are not just numerical instrument errors: researchers must also consider incorrect boundaries, missing events, biased sample sites, mistaken ages and incomplete constitutive laws.",
