          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nMineral dissolution and hydrolysis alter primary silicates and carbonates, releasing solutes and producing secondary phases; reaction progress depends on water availability and flow paths. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nColor changes, clay mineral assemblages, corestone surfaces and pore-water chemistry indicate alteration histories. Record bedrock exposure, soil composition, slope angle and water conditions. In the diagram of soil depth along one crest-to-hollow traverse and timed tracer distances, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nIron staining may reflect later groundwater flow rather than progressive weathering of all minerals. Sample an intact bedrock-to-soil profile, compare weathering indices with immobile element ratios. Consider transported colluvium mistaken for in-place weathering. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Catchment solute export\n\n**Given.** 15 metric tonnes per hectare per year from a 35 ha catchment. **Calculation:** 15 \u00d7 35. **Result:** **525 tonnes/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. If runoff chemistry changes seasonally, a single water sample is unrepresentative. Solute yield also includes atmospheric and non-weathering sources. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument parent-material discrimination; independently check water-pressure monitoring. Assess sensitivity to transported versus residual layers and verify rare-event documentation. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nA weathering index integrates parent composition and drainage as well as time. State the assumed parent rock before assigning any alteration gradient to climate. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for chemical weathering and reaction fronts without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
