          "heading": "Lesson 2.2 \u2014 Physical breakdown and regolith production",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nThermal stress, frost cracking, salt crystallization and root growth can fragment rock, but the dominant mechanism depends on climate and material. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nFracture patterns, grain-size distributions, moisture histories and fresh fragment textures constrain mechanisms. Record bedrock exposure, soil composition, slope angle and water conditions. In the diagram of soil depth along one crest-to-hollow traverse and timed tracer distances, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nAngular debris at a cliff base may result from seismic shaking rather than freeze\u2013thaw. Log fracture orientation and seasonal temperature-moisture cycles; compare sheltered and exposed microenvironments. Consider transported colluvium mistaken for in-place weathering. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Rock-fracture frequency\n\n**Given.** 18 joints intersect a 6 m scanline. **Calculation:** 18 / 6. **Result:** **3 joints/m**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. A scanline oblique to a preferred joint set produces a different apparent frequency. Frequency is not volumetric fracture density. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument parent-material discrimination; independently check water-pressure monitoring. Assess sensitivity to transported versus residual layers and verify rare-event documentation. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nTemperature oscillations alone do not establish ice-driven cracking without available liquid water. Link each proposed mechanism to a measurable environmental precondition. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for physical breakdown and regolith production without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
