          "heading": "Lesson 2.3 \u2014 Soil thickness and residence time",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nSoil thickness responds to bedrock conversion and downslope flux divergence; a steady thickness can coexist with active production and export. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nAuger transects, soil-bedrock contact roughness, tracer distributions and clast weathering show storage and transport. Record bedrock exposure, soil composition, slope angle and water conditions. In the diagram of soil depth along one crest-to-hollow traverse and timed tracer distances, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA deep colluvial hollow may reflect deposition rather than high in-place weathering rates. Measure across ridge, midslope and hollow while separating transported from residual regolith. Consider transported colluvium mistaken for in-place weathering. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Soil storage rate\n\n**Given.** Regolith production of 0.70 mm/yr and local net export of 0.45 mm/yr. **Calculation:** 0.70 \u2212 0.45. **Result:** **0.25 mm/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. If export exceeds production, regolith thins in this simplified point balance. Deposition and compaction are neglected. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument parent-material discrimination; independently check water-pressure monitoring. Assess sensitivity to transported versus residual layers and verify rare-event documentation. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nBioturbation and mixed parent materials compromise simple soil-age estimates. Treat soil depth as a balance, not a direct duration counter. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for soil thickness and residence time without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
