          "heading": "Lesson 2.4 \u2014 Hillslope creep and diffusive models",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nFor gentle slopes, sediment flux is often approximated as proportional to local surface gradient, producing systematic smoothing of topography over time. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nConvex soil-mantled crests, bent posts and tracer displacement can support slow redistribution. Record bedrock exposure, soil composition, slope angle and water conditions. In the diagram of soil depth along one crest-to-hollow traverse and timed tracer distances, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA long convex ridge may also reflect lithologic inheritance rather than active diffusion. Use an assumed transport coefficient to predict flux changes along a synthetic hillslope and compare with mapped soil depth. Consider transported colluvium mistaken for in-place weathering. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Linear hillslope sediment flux\n\n**Given.** An assumed D=0.030 m\u00b2/yr and downslope slope magnitude 0.28. **Calculation:** 0.030 \u00d7 0.28. **Result:** **0.0084 m\u00b2/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Flux doubles if the transport coefficient doubles at fixed slope. The model is a per-unit-contour-length idealization, not a debris-flow flux. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument parent-material discrimination; independently check water-pressure monitoring. Assess sensitivity to transported versus residual layers and verify rare-event documentation. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nLinear diffusion is inappropriate where slopes approach threshold conditions or landslides dominate. Give a coefficient calibration method and the range of slopes for which the model is defensible. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for hillslope creep and diffusive models without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
