          "heading": "Lesson 2.5 \u2014 Landslides and debris flows",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nSlope failure depends on material strength, pore pressure, loading and geometry; failure can transform a slide into a rapidly channelized debris flow. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nHeadscarps, displaced blocks, shear surfaces, runout deposits and rainfall records distinguish failure stages. Record bedrock exposure, soil composition, slope angle and water conditions. In the diagram of soil depth along one crest-to-hollow traverse and timed tracer distances, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA fresh debris fan can be a flood deposit rather than a hillslope failure if no source scar is documented. Map the source, transport and deposition zones and compare sorting, matrix content and levee geometry. Consider transported colluvium mistaken for in-place weathering. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Idealized stability ratio\n\n**Given.** c=6 kPa, soil unit weight 19 kN/m\u00b3, z=2 m, angle 30\u00b0, friction angle 28\u00b0, water ratio 0.40. **Calculation:** [6 + (19\u00d72\u22120.40\u00d79.81\u00d72)cos\u00b230\u00b0 tan28\u00b0] / [(19\u00d72)sin30\u00b0cos30\u00b0]. **Result:** **1.09539 ratio**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Larger pore pressure lowers resisting effective stress in this simplified calculation. Never use this classroom number for landslide forecasts or engineering design. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument parent-material discrimination; independently check water-pressure monitoring. Assess sensitivity to transported versus residual layers and verify rare-event documentation. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nIdeal infinite-slope calculations omit progressive failure, three-dimensional geometry and debris-flow rheology. Never use a classroom stability index to certify slope safety or predict exact failure timing. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for landslides and debris flows without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
