          "secNumber": "3.2",
          "heading": "Unit perspective",
          "content": "Rivers couple catchment runoff to sediment delivery, channel form and downstream deposition. The water discharge passing a cross-section is not the same as the sediment discharge and neither alone determines channel geometry. Grain entrainment depends on boundary shear stress, grain properties and shielding; available transport capacity differs from the available supply. During floods, channels can erode banks, migrate or build bars while floodplains exchange water and fine sediment. In incising reaches, longitudinal profiles contain information about uplift, changing base level and rock strength, but the same knickpoint shape can reflect several histories. Channel width, depth, roughness, slope and velocity covary. Simplified formulas are appropriate for checking units and direction of change; robust inference depends on direct field sampling and hydraulic calibration. We also distinguish contemporary event sediment pulses from longer-term terrace records. Preservation of a floodplain surface depends on deposition and later erosion, so a stratigraphic sequence is a biased archive of earlier processes.\n\n### Learning outcomes and scale discipline\n\nBy the end of this unit, students should be able to describe at least five independent landform or sediment observations, demonstrate one dimensionally consistent quantitative calculation, design a comparative measurement, and distinguish an interpretation from the observations used to support it. Practice viewing the same landform in a map, a section and a material description. Keep an explicit column for phenomena that are absent, unobserved or only assumed. At the finest scale, a few grain contacts may govern transport; at larger scales, inherited valley geometry and event histories control the response. Switching scale is legitimate only after specifying which variables remain meaningful. At the start of the field exercise, make a drawing of a downstream profile, two bar maps and time-integrated transport samples; list which parts would be measured directly and which reconstructed from other evidence.\n\nThe unit's measurement choices should include surveyed discharge section, particle size and stage observations; the strongest report also checks bankfull stage and a consistent datum. Even a carefully derived rate does not establish its formative mechanism unless the sedimentary or stratigraphic evidence agrees. Interpret changes in the context of the process and observation interval, and use at least two sources of independent evidence. Most useful scientific uncertainties are not just numerical instrument errors: researchers must also consider incorrect boundaries, missing events, biased sample sites, mistaken ages and incomplete constitutive laws.",
