          "secNumber": "3.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 3.3 \u2014 Bedload, suspension and sediment budgets",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nCoarse particles may roll or hop along the bed while fine fractions travel in suspension; the delivered load also depends on sediment availability. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nTurbidity, suspended-sediment samples, bar deposits and tracer stones reveal different pathways. Record surveyed discharge section, particle size and stage observations. In the diagram of a downstream profile, two bar maps and time-integrated transport samples, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nWater discharge peaks and sediment peaks can be asynchronous because a flood exhausts or reconnects sources. Sample sediment on rising and falling hydrograph limbs, then integrate loads with error brackets. Consider changing roughness mistaken for a change in sediment supply. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Suspended load\n\n**Given.** Suspended sediment concentration .4 kg/m\u00b3 at a discharge of 40 m\u00b3/s. **Calculation:** 0.4 \u00d7 40. **Result:** **16 kg/s**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. A daily load requires integrating concentration and discharge together over time. Instantaneous load is not a directly measured annual yield. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument hydrograph timing; independently check cross-sectional integration. Assess sensitivity to supply/transport separation and verify stage comparability. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nA high turbidity reading is not itself a calibrated mass-concentration measurement. Keep supply limitation and transport capacity conceptually separate. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for bedload, suspension and sediment budgets without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
