          "heading": "Lesson 3.4 \u2014 Meanders, bars and channel migration",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nCurvature-driven secondary circulation, bank erodibility and sediment deposition influence migration, bar development and cutoffs. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nSequential mapped centerlines, bank pins, point-bar deposits and cutoff scars constrain movement. Record surveyed discharge section, particle size and stage observations. In the diagram of a downstream profile, two bar maps and time-integrated transport samples, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA channel that changes shape after one flood may not follow its earlier average migration rate. Overlay photographs tied to common control points and compute positional uncertainty before estimating movement. Consider changing roughness mistaken for a change in sediment supply. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Lateral migration\n\n**Given.** An 8 m bank displacement measured across a five-year map interval. **Calculation:** 8 / 5. **Result:** **1.6 m/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. One flood may account for most of the motion; a mean rate hides variability. Registration error must be smaller than the apparent movement. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument hydrograph timing; independently check cross-sectional integration. Assess sensitivity to supply/transport separation and verify stage comparability. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nBank-line definitions vary with flow stage and vegetation, which can produce apparent change. State whether migration refers to bank, centerline or channel-belt boundary. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for meanders, bars and channel migration without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
