          "heading": "Lesson 3.5 \u2014 Terraces, floodplains and alluvial archives",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nA floodplain exchanges water and sediment with an active river while a terrace records a former surface disconnected by incision or uplift. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nStrath contacts, overbank silts, soil profiles, terrace elevations and dates distinguish depositional episodes. Record surveyed discharge section, particle size and stage observations. In the diagram of a downstream profile, two bar maps and time-integrated transport samples, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nTerraces at similar heights may form at different times in separate tributaries. Survey elevations relative to the modern channel and date material demonstrably tied to terrace formation. Consider changing roughness mistaken for a change in sediment supply. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Terrace incision average\n\n**Given.** 15 m difference between a terrace strath and modern channel dated to 6,000 years. **Calculation:** 15 / 6000 \u00d7 1000. **Result:** **2.5 mm/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. A younger valid terrace age implies a higher averaged incision rate. Incision rate is not automatically rock uplift rate. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument hydrograph timing; independently check cross-sectional integration. Assess sensitivity to supply/transport separation and verify stage comparability. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nReworked charcoal can be older than the floodplain deposit that encloses it. Use terrace height as geometry, not as an age measurement by itself. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for terraces, floodplains and alluvial archives without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
