          "secNumber": "4.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 4.3 \u2014 Glacial erosion and sediment transport",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nAbrasion polishes and striates rock while quarrying removes blocks; sediment can move within, under and atop ice. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nStriations, roche moutonn\u00e9e asymmetry, erratic lithology and till fabric provide complementary evidence. Record surface stake location, snow density and sedimentary facies. In the diagram of mapped moraine ridge, till section, outwash terrace and dated surface, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA polished bedrock surface may have been modified by later streams or human abrasion. Measure two aligned bedrock striation populations and compare with independently mapped erratic source regions. Consider a landslide ridge misidentified as a moraine. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Ice-transported sediment volume\n\n**Given.** Outwash sheet approximated as 200 m wide, 1,500 m long and 2 m thick. **Calculation:** 200 \u00d7 1500 \u00d7 2. **Result:** **600,000 m\u00b3**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Real sediment thickness varies; a deposit grid yields a better volume estimate. Volume does not identify depositional age. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument balance-year boundaries; independently check sediment facies. Assess sensitivity to surface versus basal movement and verify reworked material. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nStriations register local motion at one time; crossing sets imply a complex history. A single landform should not be treated as a complete ice-flow reconstruction. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for glacial erosion and sediment transport without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
