          "secNumber": "4.7",
          "heading": "Lesson 4.4 \u2014 Moraines and glaciofluvial sequences",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nIce margins can concentrate unsorted sediment in moraines, while meltwater sorts sand and gravel into outwash and terraces. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nStratigraphic contacts, clast roundness, bedding and moraine geometry constrain ice-contact and river deposition. Record surface stake location, snow density and sedimentary facies. In the diagram of mapped moraine ridge, till section, outwash terrace and dated surface, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA ridge along a valley wall may be a landslide deposit rather than a lateral moraine. Log a measured section for till matrix, bedded sand and deformation structures; map ridge continuity. Consider a landslide ridge misidentified as a moraine. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Moraine sediment mass\n\n**Given.** Illustrative 25,000 m\u00b3 deposit with assumed bulk density 1.8 tonnes/m\u00b3. **Calculation:** 25000 \u00d7 1.8. **Result:** **45,000 tonnes**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Bulk density varies with water content, porosity and grain sizes. A ridge may include non-moraine materials. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument balance-year boundaries; independently check sediment facies. Assess sensitivity to surface versus basal movement and verify reworked material. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nReworking can mix till and outwash; surface boulders may not date the ridge. Infer depositional origin from sedimentary evidence plus landscape position. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for moraines and glaciofluvial sequences without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
