          "heading": "Lesson 4.5 \u2014 Permafrost and frost-related landforms",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nGround ice, thermal contraction and seasonal freezing alter volume and strength, promoting frost heave, thaw settlement and patterned ground. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nIce-wedge casts, sorted circles, solifluction lobes and temperature profiles preserve related but distinct processes. Record surface stake location, snow density and sedimentary facies. In the diagram of mapped moraine ridge, till section, outwash terrace and dated surface, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nPolygonal cracks in drying lake mud can resemble thermal-contraction polygons. Compare polygon scale and subsurface structure with thermal and moisture records. Consider a landslide ridge misidentified as a moraine. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Active-layer deepening trend\n\n**Given.** A 0.15 m increase in seasonal thaw depth over six years. **Calculation:** 0.15 / 6 \u00d7 1000. **Result:** **25 mm/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. The rate may vary with snow cover rather than follow a steady climate trend. Two surveys cannot isolate permafrost processes. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument balance-year boundaries; independently check sediment facies. Assess sensitivity to surface versus basal movement and verify reworked material. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nNot every cold-region slope has permafrost, and relict forms can persist after warming. Separate modern ground-temperature measurement from interpretation of inherited morphology. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for permafrost and frost-related landforms without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
