          "secNumber": "5.5",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.2 \u2014 Dunes, ripples and migration",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nSand transport across a dune creates erosion on the windward face and deposition near the lee slope; dune shape integrates wind regime and sediment supply. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nRepeat crest surveys, cross bedding, grain sorting and satellite time series reveal active migration. Record sediment source, wind regime, storm rainfall and surface crust. In the diagram of dune crests, fan lobes, loess exposures and playa salt chemistry, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA stationary crest can mask internal sediment flux if opposing winds balance net displacement. Measure crest movement relative to fixed benchmarks and compute velocity over the known interval. Consider dust provenance attributed to a local surface without trajectory evidence. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Dune crest migration\n\n**Given.** Crest shifts 22 m downwind over 11 years. **Calculation:** 22 / 11. **Result:** **2 m/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Migration can be irregular under seasonally reversing winds. A shifting crest alone does not quantify sand flux. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument surface crust; independently check wind direction. Assess sensitivity to storm-runoff episodes and verify provenance. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nMigration scaling by flux divided by dune height ignores three-dimensional leakage and variable supply. Use simplified migration formulas for comparison, not site-specific forecasts. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for dunes, ripples and migration without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
