          "heading": "Lesson 5.3 \u2014 Alluvial fans and ephemeral channels",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nShort intense floods and debris flows distribute sediment from confined mountain valleys across unconfined basin margins, producing distributary lobes. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nLobe boundaries, channel avulsions, matrix support, sorting and surface varnish constrain active and abandoned sectors. Record sediment source, wind regime, storm rainfall and surface crust. In the diagram of dune crests, fan lobes, loess exposures and playa salt chemistry, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA fan surface may contain deposits from both streamflow and debris flows within a single storm history. Map facies and channel patterns along apex-to-toe transects before assigning one dominant transport style. Consider dust provenance attributed to a local surface without trajectory evidence. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Alluvial fan gradient\n\n**Given.** Fan elevation falls 160 m over 4,000 m horizontal distance. **Calculation:** 100 \u00d7 160 / 4000. **Result:** **4 %**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. An individual channel gradient can differ from the whole fan surface. A gradient cannot uniquely identify debris flow versus streamflow. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument surface crust; independently check wind direction. Assess sensitivity to storm-runoff episodes and verify provenance. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nFlood recurrence statistics cannot be inferred reliably from a single preserved fan deposit. Integrate sediment facies with upstream catchment and storm information. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for alluvial fans and ephemeral channels without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
