          "heading": "Lesson 5.4 \u2014 Playas, desert pavements and evaporites",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nClosed basins collect intermittent runoff; evaporation precipitates salts, while deflation and lag formation can armor nearby surfaces. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nMud cracks, graded lake beds, evaporite crusts and pavement clast fabrics constrain environmental episodes. Record sediment source, wind regime, storm rainfall and surface crust. In the diagram of dune crests, fan lobes, loess exposures and playa salt chemistry, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nSurface salinity can result from irrigation return flow rather than an ancient lake stage. Core a short basin transect and analyze salts, grain size and sedimentary structures. Consider dust provenance attributed to a local surface without trajectory evidence. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Salt concentration mass balance\n\n**Given.** A brine volume of 2 million L holds dissolved salts at 3 g/L. **Calculation:** 2000000 \u00d7 3 / 1000. **Result:** **6,000 kg**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Evaporation concentrates solute without necessarily increasing total dissolved mass. Precipitation and mineral reactions must be considered. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument surface crust; independently check wind direction. Assess sensitivity to storm-runoff episodes and verify provenance. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nEvaporite assemblages are affected by groundwater and post-depositional dissolution. Distinguish depositional record from later diagenesis and modern salinization. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for playas, desert pavements and evaporites without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
