          "secNumber": "5.8",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.5 \u2014 Loess and dryland environmental archives",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nWind-deposited silt blankets can record dust transport, soil formation and climate changes across broad intervals. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nGrain-size shifts, palaeosols, luminescence dates and magnetic susceptibility supply related age and process clues. Record sediment source, wind regime, storm rainfall and surface crust. In the diagram of dune crests, fan lobes, loess exposures and playa salt chemistry, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA silt bed can be overbank alluvium instead of loess if sedimentary structure is ignored. Log contacts and soil horizons, sample provenance and assess the depositional setting outside the vertical section. Consider dust provenance attributed to a local surface without trajectory evidence. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Loess sediment accumulation\n\n**Given.** A 1.4 m loess layer accumulated across an idealized 20,000 years. **Calculation:** 1.4 / 20000 \u00d7 1000. **Result:** **0.07 mm/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Palaeosols may represent intervals of little net accumulation. Compaction means thickness change is not original deposited volume. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument surface crust; independently check wind direction. Assess sensitivity to storm-runoff episodes and verify provenance. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nLuminescence ages require sufficient resetting and palaeosols may represent time-averaged processes. Pair chronology with sedimentology before reconstructing a regional wind regime. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for loess and dryland environmental archives without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
