          "secNumber": "6.8",
          "heading": "Lesson 6.5 \u2014 Cliff erosion, coastal hazards and adaptation",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nWave attack, weathering, rainfall-driven failures and geology combine in cliff retreat, often producing episodic rather than uniform change. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nHistoric cliff-top positions, debris aprons, discontinuities and groundwater seepage constrain drivers. Record wave direction, tidal datum, survey dates and sediment sources. In the diagram of repeat profile, mapped shoreline and bounded littoral sediment budget, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nAn apparent cliff retreat line may be a mapping artifact caused by changing image angle and vegetation. Register multiple maps to stable control points and compare rates across distinct observation intervals. Consider beach seasonal exchange mistaken for permanent coastal erosion. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Mean cliff-top retreat\n\n**Given.** Historical cliff-top map positions differ by 14 m over 35 years. **Calculation:** 14 / 35. **Result:** **0.4 m/yr**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Cliff failure is episodic; a trend cannot predict next collapse. Map positional uncertainties must be estimated. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument datum consistency; independently check sediment cell boundaries. Assess sensitivity to tidal timing and verify land-level motion. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nSimple linear extrapolation misses rare collapses and uncertain future forcing. Classroom recession rates do not authorize site-specific hazard setbacks. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for cliff erosion, coastal hazards and adaptation without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
