          "secNumber": "7.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 7.1 \u2014 Carbonate dissolution and karst drainage",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nCarbonate dissolution follows water chemistry and pathways through fractures, enlarging conduits that can bypass surface divides. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nSinkholes, disappearing streams, caves, springs and dye-tracer travel times constrain underground connectivity. Record bedrock solubility, uplift controls and crosscutting deposits. In the diagram of spring chemistry, terrace age, lava-flow chronology and valley profile, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA circular depression can be human excavation or subsidence above buried infrastructure rather than karst. Compare bedrock geology, depression morphology and groundwater tracer results within a safety-approved investigation plan. Consider an apparent uplift surface actually formed by lithologic resistance. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Karst solute flux\n\n**Given.** A spring discharges .5 m\u00b3/s and its solute mass concentration exceeds a reference by .060 kg/m\u00b3. **Calculation:** .5 \u00d7 .060. **Result:** **0.03 kg/s**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. A changing source mixture can change measured concentrations independent of rock dissolution. This is a screening export estimate, not pure limestone removal. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument groundwater networks; independently check age inheritance. Assess sensitivity to rock resistance and verify stratigraphic overprint. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nSubsurface pathways shift with water level and conduits cannot be mapped confidently from contours alone. Do not assume surface flow paths describe contaminant movement in karst. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for carbonate dissolution and karst drainage without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
          "simulations": []
