          "heading": "Lesson 7.3 \u2014 Volcanic construction and erosion",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nLava and tephra construct topography; rain, rivers and mass wasting quickly redistribute unconsolidated deposits, including possible lahars. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nFlow margins, ash beds, drainage diversion and reworked volcaniclastic strata reveal sequence. Record bedrock solubility, uplift controls and crosscutting deposits. In the diagram of spring chemistry, terrace age, lava-flow chronology and valley profile, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nA radial drainage pattern may form on a volcanic cone or another approximately conical mountain. Map superposition of lava, ash and stream channels and classify sediment textures by depositional process. Consider an apparent uplift surface actually formed by lithologic resistance. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Volcaniclastic deposit volume\n\n**Given.** An area of 2 km\u00b2 receives an average 0.30 m loose deposit. **Calculation:** 2e6 \u00d7 0.30. **Result:** **600,000 m\u00b3**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Spatial thickness measurements should replace a blanket mean in practice. Compaction and erosion change current volume. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument groundwater networks; independently check age inheritance. Assess sensitivity to rock resistance and verify stratigraphic overprint. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nAn extinct landscape form provides no direct prediction of present eruption probability. Separate volcanic landform origin from current hazard assessment. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for volcanic construction and erosion without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
