          "heading": "Lesson 7.4 \u2014 Rock uplift and climate in relief production",
          "content": "### Process mechanism and scale\n\nRelief can grow when uplift or base-level forcing outpaces denudation, but precipitation, vegetation and rock strength affect erosion efficiency. Draw a control volume showing forcing, material transfers and storage. Name the scale and timescale of the proposed process; landform shape alone does not reveal either.\n\n### Observable evidence\n\nLow-temperature thermochronology, river profiles, sediment yields and geological structure provide scale-specific evidence. Record bedrock solubility, uplift controls and crosscutting deposits. In the diagram of spring chemistry, terrace age, lava-flow chronology and valley profile, label measured features separately from inferred histories. Provide survey dates, scale and a detection threshold.\n\n### Alternatives and field tests\n\nHigh relief can persist due to resistant rock without rapid contemporary uplift. Compare two hypothetical catchments with differing lithology under the same imposed uplift scenario. Consider an apparent uplift surface actually formed by lithologic resistance. Identify one independently measurable outcome that would distinguish the two interpretations, and one negative result that would undermine the leading explanation.\n\n### Worked quantitative example \u2014 Competing relief budgets\n\n**Given.** Simplified 100,000-year uplift at 1.7 mm/yr and 130 m of cumulative lowering. **Calculation:** 1.7 \u00d7 100000 / 1000 \u2212 130. **Result:** **40 m**. Work through the units before using the output to discuss Earth-surface history. Greater denudation could remove all net uplift-induced elevation gain. Surface elevation and relief are not synonyms. A correct result from an incorrect conceptual model is not evidence that the process operated. State which measured variable would need independent verification. If the answer is a rate, indicate the averaging interval; if it is a geometric ratio, identify the scale and measurement direction; if it is a budget, give the boundary and the sign convention.\n\n### Research procedure and critical interpretation\n\nDocument groundwater networks; independently check age inheritance. Assess sensitivity to rock resistance and verify stratigraphic overprint. Record each raw observation, units, uncertainty and assumptions before model fitting.\n\nErosion laws have calibrated exponents; similar topography may arise from dissimilar histories. Avoid a one-to-one equation between mountain height and uplift rate. A high-resolution shape map without ages and a dated deposit without spatial coverage offer different constraints. Explain how they could be combined for rock uplift and climate in relief production without overclaiming a process history. Identify one further test.\n\n### Problems with answer guidance\n\n**Problem 1.** Recalculate the worked case after changing an input by 20% and show units. **Problem 2.** Contrast two possible landform origins using a specific field test. **Answer guidance:** correct units, explicit physical assumptions and a genuinely distinguishing observation are essential.",
